   296	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   297	    """
   298	    Grow straight rays from framed 2x2 blocks; any foreground pixel, including other green frames, halts the ray.
   299	
   300	    Input structure:
   301	    - The background is the most common color in the grid.
   302	    - Each object is a monochrome 2x2 block inside a shared green 4x4 frame.
   303	    - Every frame contains one or more structural border gaps made of background cells.
   304	
   305	    Rule:
   306	    - Each gap is recolored with its block's color and emits one outward cardinal or diagonal ray according to the gap position.
   307	    - Rays advance synchronously through background only; the next step immediately stops a ray if it leaves the grid or if the destination is any non-background pixel.
   308	    - Obstacles include other green frame walls, inner 2x2 blocks, and ray pixels painted earlier; if multiple rays try to enter the same empty cell on the same step, none of them paints that cell.
   309	
   310	    Key insight:
   311	    - The important new pattern is that frames have a dual role: they still define beam sources through their gaps, but their intact green border cells also act as hard blockers that truncate neighboring rays.
   312	    """
   313	    return _simulate(grid, collect_stats=False)
